    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Leonardo was working on a portrait of Lisa del Giocondo by October 1503, but he never gave the painting to her family, and he may have continued to work on it as late as 1517.</li><li>The portrait was one of the first Italian portraits to set the sitter before an imaginary landscape, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a> borrowed from its composition within a few years.</li><li>It passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I of France</a>, hung in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> from 1797 and became widely known partly through its theft in 1911 by Vincenzo Peruggia, who believed it should belong to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A marginal note of October 1503 in a 1477 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>, found in 2005 by a scholar at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-university">Heidelberg University</a>, likens Leonardo to the Greek painter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apelles">Apelles</a> and says he was then working on a portrait of Lisa del Giocondo.</p></aside>
    </section>

